00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:37.809
The little ciliate you see swimming through
the field of debris on your screen right now has a fun name.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:41.140
It’s called Rhinothrix porculus.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:46.178
The first part of the name, Rhinothrix, combines
the Greek words “rhino”, for nose,

00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:48.110
and “thrix”, for hair.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:51.660
No, the name is not an ode to nose hair.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:57.180
Rather, the name is a reference to the hair-like
cilia around the organism, and the fact that

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.000
up there at one end, is what
looks like a teeny tiny nose.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.110
The second part of the organism’s name,
”porculus”, translates to pig.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:10.770
And that makes sense because Rhinothrix porculus
likes to live in mud.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.660
This little organism belongs to the family
Spathidiidae, though we’ll just call them

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.726
by their common name Spathidiids.

00:01:18.726 --> 00:01:24.890
Spathidiids are found in freshwater and marine
habitats, but they’re also often found in soil.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:31.390
And some time ago, James, our master of microscopes,
received 60 soil samples from a far away forest

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.780
on the eastern edge of Poland, storing them
in plastic bottles in his bathroom.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.970
And every so often, like a force of nature
gathering over a woodland, James adds water

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:46.520
to those samples and watches the organisms
go wild.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.970
And wild do they go.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:52.090
This is a recording of one of their feeding
frenzies, which we think involves a horde

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.775
of Spathidiids wreaking havoc on a population
of ciliates called colpodids.

00:01:57.775 --> 00:02:02.340
It’s chaos in microbial form and if you’re
having trouble making out exactly what’s

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.910
going on, don’t worry, that’s kind of
the Spathidiid experience for you.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:15.090
The family Spathidiidae is made up of around
20 genera, which encompass around 250 known species.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.887
And there’s a lot of variety in the Spathidiid
family to sort through.

00:02:18.887 --> 00:02:24.070
For example, he largest species is about 35 
times larger than the smallest.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:30.140
And as far as shape goes, well, their name
is a callback to the origins of the word “spatula,”

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:36.700
and you can see that spatula shape in some of the species
 when the broadness of its front end comes into view.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.660
But that is not universal.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.780
Some species are more round, 
others more cylindrical.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.943
And others take on shapes that require words
like “botuliform”

00:02:45.943 --> 00:02:49.400
and “lunate” and “turbinate” to describe them.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.710
And all Spathidiids are fairly flexible, which
helps them bend and wiggle around whatever

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.640
obstacle the microcosmos throws their way.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:03.308
They can glide along mud particles, but in
water, they can also swim, by spinning around.

00:03:07.958 --> 00:03:13.130
Some Spathidiids though put their flexibility
to work in other forms of movement, like this

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:19.490
species ducking in and out of the substrate
in motions that look more like a worm than

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.750
the spinning ciliate we saw earlier.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:28.680
As diverse as Spathidiids are, there are a
few helpful features they all have in common.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.350
One is that large circle you see at the back
of their body.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:38.510
That’s the contractile vacuole, which helps
control the amount of fluid inside the organism.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:44.050
As they swim through the water, the Spathidiids
accumulate water and other fluid in their bodies.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:49.520
And while water is, you know, essential, if
there is too much, the Spathidiid will burst.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:56.069
So to avoid that, watery explosion, they rely
on those large contractile vacuoles to gather

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:00.170
excess fluid and then expel it back out into
the world.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:04.840
So in a way, what you’re looking at is the
Spathidiid equivalent of a bladder, taking

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:10.267
up a large amount of space so that the ciliate
can do its own version of peeing.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:15.718
Spathidiids are colorless, but as you can
see here, they sometimes take on some green coloring.

00:04:15.718 --> 00:04:20.949
That’s not their own pigments though—they
take on the verdant hue thanks to algae that

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:26.860
have either taken up residence as a symbiont,
or that have had the misfortune to be eaten.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:31.694
In some cases, like with our Rhinothrix from
earlier, you can also see

00:04:31.694 --> 00:04:35.270
bright, shimmering lights glowing from within.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:40.600
Some of those are crystalline parts of the
organism, while others are sugars that refract

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.810
beautifully under our microscope’s light.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:51.169
To hunt down their food, Spathidiids rely
on the toxicysts lining their mouths to immobilize their prey.

00:04:51.169 --> 00:04:56.110
And from there, some species will swallow
their meals whole, while others use chemicals

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:01.393
released by their toxicysts to mash up their
meals outside their body,

00:05:01.393 --> 00:05:05.219
like a little protist smoothie 
they can just slurp up.

00:05:05.219 --> 00:05:10.080
Now for all the distinctive traits of these
different species, they’re quite difficult

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.620
to identify by just looking at them
under a microscope.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.589
Many of the traits that differentiate them
are subtle, rooted in features that don’t

00:05:17.589 --> 00:05:20.479
immediately reveal themselves to us.

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:25.569
Instead, scientists have to turn to other
methods to bring those traits to light.

00:05:25.569 --> 00:05:31.220
Take this species, for example, which we’re
pretty sure belongs to the genus Protospathidium.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:36.639
One way for us to know for sure is to look
for a specific part unique to the genus, but

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:43.039
we can’t see it without killing the organisms
and adding stains that highlight that part.

00:05:43.039 --> 00:05:48.310
So that’s why so much of our Spathidiid
footage is so vaguely labeled, it's hard to

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:52.461
figure out exactly what they are without killing
them, and we don’t really want to do that.

00:05:52.461 --> 00:05:57.240
But that doesn’t mean we can’t enjoy digging
through their lives.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.550
And there are a few species that we have been
able to identify a bit further, like this

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.319
moving ball of fuzz, which James is pretty
sure belongs to the genus Penardiella.

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:10.590
In fact, it might even be completely new.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:15.569
When James first saw it under the microscope,
he thought it must be a damaged cell.

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:21.030
But he was shocked to find many more like
it on his slide, so now he’s collecting

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:26.194
more data to see whether or not it really
is a new species.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.550
For comparison, this is another member of
the Penardiella genus that we think is Penardiella undulata.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:42.889
If we’re correct, this would be the first
time someone has documented the species in

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:45.000
almost 90 years!

00:06:48.049 --> 00:06:53.289
This species belongs to the genus Perispira,
but there’s not much known about it except

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:58.419
that its mouth spirals around its body, which
is definitely a helpful trait when it comes

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:00.610
to identifying the species.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:06.099
And James is doing more molecular work with
this species to see if he can unravel more

00:07:06.099 --> 00:07:07.869
of its mysteries.

00:07:07.869 --> 00:07:13.867
But until then, the Spathidiids remain just
about as murky and opaque as the mud they

00:07:13.867 --> 00:07:19.056
come from, though perhaps just a little bit
more beautiful to look at.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:28.639
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

